_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$\n\nII. $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=2$\n\nIII. $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\})=3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430827 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "48a8881c-e6ac-54a2-9375-24bf5efbb3a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430827 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}.$ Moreover, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ are linearly independent vectors. So,  ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=3$ (not $2$).\n\n- Statement III is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\})=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430828 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186430825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430828 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35300" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430828 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430828 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430828 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6f3e3a92-ac43-52ed-a6de-e1847c3b16d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430828 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 6 & -9 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430829 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186430824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430829 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79321" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430829 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430829 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the dimension of $\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430829 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fd38fa95-000b-54ff-9226-2d221d8be579" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430829 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
